J. A ripple at two radii. The equation says the part with m waves at an inner radius is the same part at an outer one, shrunk by (inner/outer)^(m^2/3), plus what was gathered between:
   so its two amplitudes (the cos and the sin) are each correlated across clouds by (inner/outer)^(m^2/3) x sqrt(g_m(outer) / g_m(inner)), with no constant in it. Measured (standard error from splitting the clouds in ten) and predicted:
   exactly n =   100000, 1 wave:  0.8 and 0.3: 0.441 (0.008), 0.431;  0.9 and 0.5: 0.410 (0.009), 0.390;  0.6 and 0.2: 0.568 (0.007), 0.563;  0.5 and 0.4: 0.877 (0.002), 0.870
